Lateral Epicondylitis (Tennis Elbow)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Lateral epicondyle localized pain, Pain exacerbated by resisted wrist extension, Weakened grip strength

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-028
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, sharp elbow pain radiating to the forearm, causing a sudden, total inability to grip or lift objects with the dominant hand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing an agonizing flare of elbow pain following heavy domestic manual labor, resulting in sudden grip failure and the inability to hold basic items like a kettle or door handle. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to differentiate an acute extensor tendon rupture or cervical radiculopathy from severe lateral epicondylitis, and to implement immediate mechanical offloading to restore safe, basic hand function prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My elbow feels like it's on fire, and the pain shoots right down my arm; I just tried to pick up my coffee mug and my hand completely gave way."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right lateral elbow pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of a dull, aching pain localized strictly over the lateral epicondyle present for 6 weeks; drastically worsened over the last 12 hours after a prolonged session of aggressive hedge-trimming. Currently reports excruciating pain radiating distally along the extensor muscle mass of the dorsal forearm. The patient describes a sudden, profound weakness in grip strength secondary to pain inhibition, dropping several lightweight objects today. Pain is instantly triggered by everyday activities such as gripping, turning a doorknob, or extending the wrist.
Functional Status: Impaired fine and gross motor function of the dominant hand due to pain-induced yielding; unable to perform basic self-care tasks involving grip.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of thermal injury (dropped hot beverages) due to unpredictable grip failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated, holding the right forearm securely against the torso to prevent wrist movement.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals no visible swelling, erythema, or olecranon bursitis. Exquisite, focal point tenderness palpated precisely 1 to 2 centimeters distal to the lateral epicondyle at the origin of the extensor carpi radialis brevis. Distinctly positive Cozen's test: severe pain provoked over the epicondyle during resisted active wrist extension with the elbow stabilized in full extension. Positive Mill's test: pain provocation during passive wrist flexion with the elbow in full extension. Neurological examination is completely intact; 5/5 motor strength in all other myotomes, no focal sensory loss, and normal symmetrical reflexes in the upper extremity, effectively ruling out radicular pathology. Pulses are intact.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of recent heavy manual yard work visible outside.
Medication Reconciliation: Applied topical heat and took paracetamol with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Lateral Epicondylitis (Tennis Elbow) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cervical Radiculopathy (C6/C7) - Ruled out clinically by the completely normal sensory and reflex examination of the upper limb, negative Spurling's test, and pain strictly reproduced by localized mechanical provocation of the wrist extensors.

Extensor Tendon Rupture - Ruled out by the preserved, albeit intensely painful, ability to actively extend the wrist against gravity, and absence of a palpable defect at the common extensor origin.

Radial Tunnel Syndrome - Ruled out by maximal tenderness being strictly localized to the epicondyle rather than 4-5 cm distally over the mobile wad, and pain elicited by wrist extension rather than resisted forearm supination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, highly painful exacerbation of lateral epicondylitis following a significant mechanical overload event. The critical feature was the sudden, pain-inhibited loss of grip strength, rendering the dominant hand functionally useless and unsafe for basic tasks. Postponing medical evaluation would have exposed the patient to unmanaged severe pain and the risk of secondary injuries from dropping objects. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the localized tendinopathic nature of the pain, ruled out cervical nerve root compression or structural tendon rupture, and permitted the immediate application of targeted pharmacological and mechanical offloading strategies to restore a baseline level of safe function.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 7 days to manage the acute inflammatory flare.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly lift all objects with the palm facing upward (supinated) for the next 7 days. Consequence: Complete mechanical bypassing of the damaged extensor tendons, allowing the patient to lift objects securely without triggering the sharp, yielding pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply an ice pack directly to the bony bump on the outside of the elbow for 15 minutes after any mandatory hand use. Consequence: Rapid constriction of the inflamed tissue, providing immediate, soothing numbness and reducing the deep, throbbing ache (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for eccentric strengthening protocol once the acute pain subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a counterforce elbow brace (epicondylitis clasp) positioned 2 cm distal to the epicondyle.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to monitor progress. Re-evaluate for ultrasound imaging or corticosteroid/PRP injection if refractory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the elbow becomes diffusely swollen, hot, and red, if a fever develops, or if there is a sudden, total inability to lift the wrist or fingers (wrist drop).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, reflecting tendon micro-tearing and inflammation. Imaging is usually reserved for chronic, non-healing cases. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will improve over 1-2 weeks with the brace and modified lifting, but the underlying tendon healing (tendinosis) can take 6-12 months of dedicated rehabilitation to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the entire elbow becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you completely lose the ability to lift your hand backward.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tendons that lift your wrist back have been severely strained where they attach to the bone at your elbow, causing micro-tears and intense inflammation. The counterforce brace acts like a new, artificial anchor point, spreading the pulling force away from the damaged area. The anti-inflammatories will quiet the chemical pain signals.
Actionable Steps: Do not lift anything with your palm facing down. Always lift underhand (palm up); this uses the biceps and flexors, giving the damaged extensors a complete rest. Wear the brace firmly during any gripping tasks.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe Tennis Elbow (inflamed wrist tendons). Fitted with a counterforce brace. Lift all objects strictly with palm facing UP. Take prescribed anti-inflammatories. See GP in 2 weeks.

